WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.493
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.195 --> 00:00:08.070
EUGENE LIDERMAN: Before
I dive into the talk,

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:11.130
I kind of want explain
the team and the strategy

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.830
and what we focus on.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.270
So first and foremost,
we're dedicated to improving

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.565
the security and privacy for
Android's 2 billion-plus users,

00:00:18.565 --> 00:00:19.200
all right?

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.570
And we have three pillars
that we kind of focus on.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.765
So first and foremost, it's
layered security, right?

00:00:23.765 --> 00:00:25.140
I mean, you hear
it all the time,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.820
defense in depth, the onion,
layered security model.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.980
So everything we do,
we try to think of it

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.940
as we want to use as much of the
hardware-based features we can.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.820
We always try to lock
things down in the OS

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.620
with regards to apps,
sandboxing, and APIs,

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.870
and then we always try to
we add additional controls

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.203
for app-related security, right?

00:00:44.203 --> 00:00:46.860
And then we try
really hard-- it's

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.140
part of our ethos to be as
transparent and as open as

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.540
possible with our ecosystem,
with our partners,

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.070
with our customers,
with the user base.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.950
And so we do that in a
couple of different ways.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.430
First, we-- obviously
Android is open source.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.370
We publish-- I mean, you
see every commit we make.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.285
So people can see
oftentimes people

00:01:04.285 --> 00:01:05.910
write about the new
changes because you

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.800
can see the commits that are
added to Android Open Source.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.120
Second, we publish
a lot of blogs.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.660
I think for security
specifically,

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.460
we published like
30 blogs last year.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.540
And it was-- it's
really twofold.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.040
It's one, to educate developers
about all these new security

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.540
features they can
take advantage of,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.670
and the second part is it's
to educate the research

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.280
community about the
strides that we're making.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.900
So we're continuously
publishing blogs

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.270
about new features, new
functionality, new techniques

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.950
that we're taking advantage of.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.410
We also publish a quarterly
transparency report,

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.170
which is kind of like a state
of the ecosystem in terms

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.890
of health around malware
and potentially harmful--

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.310
what we refer to as potentially
harmful applications.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.523
And then we also publish a
monthly security bulletin.

00:01:44.523 --> 00:01:47.190
So you can see what we're fixing
every month with these security

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:47.970
bulletins.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.680
And we actually also publish--

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.540
not to get too long on this,
but an annual year in review

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.920
as well, which is like
a recap of everything

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:53.910
I just covered already.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.752
So we try to provide
a lot of information.

00:01:55.752 --> 00:01:57.210
We want to educate
people on what's

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.550
going on with regard to
Android and the ecosystem.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.190
And the last piece is we
leverage the rest of Google.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:07.020
So Google's got tremendous
security and privacy expertise.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.320
Security reviews are done
in-house collectively.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.560
Incident management is done
internally collectively.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.690
We partner with the
Safe Browsing team,

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:17.940
which we'll cover a bit later.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.730
We work really closely
with the Identity team--

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:25.710
so around advanced protection,
leveraging like hardware keys

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.270
on mobile devices.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.263
We leverage insights
on machine learning

00:02:29.263 --> 00:02:31.180
and artificial intelligence
into our products,

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.097
so we get a lot of the
knowledge from the rest

00:02:33.097 --> 00:02:36.350
of the other organizations
within Google.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.850
And so in terms of our actual
team, the Security and Privacy

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.840
team, it's broken down into
three distinct subgroups.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.590
The first is the Google
Play Protect team.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.840
So this is our built-in
anti-malware protection

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:46.740
solution.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.160
It's really to protect
against third party

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.410
harmful, malicious
applications and content.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.273
It also provides
a user experience

00:02:55.273 --> 00:02:57.690
on the client side where a
user can launch the application

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.920
and kind of see their
posture in terms

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.920
of the status of how many
apps have been scanned

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.780
and your kind of
security posture.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.860
Second, we have our
platform engineering team.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:06.810
This team is the
one that actually

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.100
implements a lot of
the security features

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.240
into Android Open Source.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.185
So kind of like I
mentioned before,

00:03:12.185 --> 00:03:14.310
continuously leveraging
the hardware-based security

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:18.450
features of the device,
enhancements and sandboxing,

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.755
API hardening, kernel
updates and so forth.

00:03:20.755 --> 00:03:23.130
And then the third team is
the Android Security Assurance

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:23.703
team.

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:26.370
This team's purpose is really to
release the security bulletins.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.328
They handle the security
development lifecycle,

00:03:28.328 --> 00:03:30.150
they approve all
security-related features,

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:31.410
they do all the reviews.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.547
And they don't just do
this for AOSP and Pixel,

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:36.130
they do this for the
entire ecosystem.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.463
So they work with the system
on chip vendors, the device

00:03:38.463 --> 00:03:41.570
manufacturers, the carriers,
the research community.

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.612
So we have a large
footprint in terms

00:03:43.612 --> 00:03:45.570
of what we impact from
the security and privacy

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.513
perspective.

00:03:47.513 --> 00:03:49.680
So I tried to change things
up for this presentation

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.020
and think about it in a way
like-- usually security is

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:53.380
something where it's
very transparent,

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:54.213
it's under the hood.

00:03:54.213 --> 00:03:55.680
Good security you
don't recognize,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.305
so I wanted to show
in a different lens

00:03:57.305 --> 00:03:59.722
where some things are still
under the hood but some things

00:03:59.722 --> 00:04:00.900
I want to cover that--

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:02.550
even on your devices you
can take a look if you're--

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.680
By the way, how many
Android users are out here?

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.388
Who actually use
Android as their device,

00:04:06.388 --> 00:04:07.920
not that they
support internally?

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.350
That's right, that's right,
that's not a trick question--

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:10.650
yeah, I see you.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:11.440
Just kidding.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:12.700
[LAUGHS]

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:13.200
OK, cool.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.492
That's good to know, thank you.

00:04:14.492 --> 00:04:16.290
I'm not picking on
you, I'm just kidding.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.089
OK, so what I really
want to cover,

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:19.860
it's broken down into
four distinct groups

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:21.130
and I'll explain
them really quickly.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:22.810
So system-- in terms of
the operating system,

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.200
this is like stuff people can
exfiltrate off the device,

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.070
like a malicious attack--

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.612
not the end users
themselves in terms

00:04:27.612 --> 00:04:30.125
of introducing known risk, but
something-- a malicious stack

00:04:30.125 --> 00:04:33.360
will try to do exfiltrate
off the device.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.715
There's apps-- there's two
kinds of categories there.

00:04:35.715 --> 00:04:37.770
There's kind of
the app developers

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.918
that just make mistakes
and they build faulty apps

00:04:39.918 --> 00:04:41.460
because they're not
security experts,

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.010
and there's app developers
that actually purposely come up

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:46.170
with malicious
applications and they're

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:49.290
trying to somehow steal data
or fool the user, right?

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.300
Malware-related stuff.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.937
Third is phishing, which
I think is probably

00:04:53.937 --> 00:04:55.770
one of the more critical
things because it's

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.480
ubiquitous across devices.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.950
And it's not really related
to the OS or the technology

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:01.492
of the OS, per se,
but it's something

00:05:01.492 --> 00:05:04.397
that just applies whatever,
via email, SMS, and so forth.

00:05:04.397 --> 00:05:06.730
And then the fourth one I
want to cover is the end user.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.792
And it sounds a little
redundant because like,

00:05:08.792 --> 00:05:10.750
don't all of these impact
the end user somehow?

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:12.792
Well yes, but the first
three are kind of like,

00:05:12.792 --> 00:05:13.750
these are things that--

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.400
kind of think of
it as like these

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.880
are things that attack the user,
external sources trying to get

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.080
information from the user.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.990
And the end user themselves
is kind of like--

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:24.220
well shoot, end users kind
of create their own risk.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:25.810
They do a lot of
self-infliction.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:27.877
A lot of IT people
in the room, I'm

00:05:27.877 --> 00:05:29.710
sure everybody here is
thinking the end user

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:30.543
is the weakest link.

00:05:30.543 --> 00:05:31.668
I don't want to think that.

00:05:31.668 --> 00:05:33.310
I think very
positively, and I think

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:35.530
that with proper
education and guidance,

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.310
end users can go a long way.

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:39.820
So diving into the
system, thinking of things

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:43.030
like potentially rooting the
device or somebody trying

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.810
to pull information off
an encrypted device,

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:50.380
recovering keys off
an encrypted device,

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.728
converting bugs into exploits,
network-related security issues

00:05:53.728 --> 00:05:55.520
like man in the middle,
man in the browser.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.500
So starting with-- like I
mentioned, the secure hardware,

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.500
it all starts with secure
hardware with Android.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:04.550
So Android heavily relies on the
trusted execution environment

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:05.640
on these devices.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.820
So anyone that's not
familiar with the TEE,

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:12.620
it's basically a secure OS that
runs parallel to the Android OS

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:14.360
on the same
application processor,

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.850
but it's segregated using
software and hardware

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:18.230
from the actual Android OS.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:19.340
So it's like--

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:22.130
Android OS is the rich user
OS, TEE is a much more slimmed

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:23.810
down, lockdown OS.

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.270
And it's used for a lot of the
critical security features.

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:30.110
So just as an example, it's used
for key generation, signing,

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:31.220
encryption, decryption.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.590
It's used for secure lock
screen, pin verification when

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.810
you type in your
pin, and of course,

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:39.040
the encryption on the
device, whether it's

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:41.340
a file-based encryption
and so forth.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.000
It's also used for
version binding.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.440
So if you generate key
material in a newer OS

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.960
and like a malicious attack
would try to downgrade that OS,

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.198
those keys would not be
available on an older OS.

00:06:50.198 --> 00:06:52.490
And the same can be said for
rollback prevention, which

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:54.380
was added in Android Oreo.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:55.880
And rollback
prevention is basically

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:59.957
where once you're at a certain
OS or security patch level,

00:06:59.957 --> 00:07:01.790
somebody could not
downgrade that to a lower

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:03.855
level to be able to-- if
there was a known issue

00:07:03.855 --> 00:07:05.730
or exploit that they
could take advantage of,

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.123
it wouldn't be able to do that.

00:07:07.123 --> 00:07:09.540
And of course, Verified Boot
is also equally as important.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:11.810
Verified Boot is basically
if you think of malware,

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.460
the first thing a piece
of malware would try to do

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.560
is modify the system
partition and ensure that it

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:16.790
can persist after a reboot.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:19.910
And with Verified Boot, when
the device is booting up,

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:23.630
it cryptographically verifies
every piece of the boot process

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:25.430
step by step and if
something bad happens,

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:26.640
it alerts the user.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.156
And in some cases, if there's
two copies of the OS, which

00:07:29.156 --> 00:07:30.573
we'll cover a
little later, it can

00:07:30.573 --> 00:07:31.880
revert to the known good copy.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:35.300
And something that we
added in Android Pie

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:36.840
is tamper-resistant hardware.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.940
So with Pie now, we basically
have the support where app

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.440
developers-- and I'll cover
some of this in-depth later on--

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:44.925
can basically store key
material in discrete,

00:07:44.925 --> 00:07:46.550
tamper-resistant
hardware-- think of it

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:47.600
as a secure element.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.530
It's a separate ship separate
from the application processor,

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:55.670
and it's got its own CPU
memory and flash storage.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:58.400
And it's basically-- it
provides a higher assurance.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.860
So it's impervious
or protects better

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.610
against physical attacks, side
channel attacks, and so forth,

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:05.460
OK?

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:09.333
So moving up outside of TEE and
the tamper-resistant hardware,

00:08:09.333 --> 00:08:11.750
Android starts with the Android
kernel, which of course is

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:13.250
built off the Linux kernel.

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:17.180
Since Android 4.4, SELinux has
implemented mandatory access

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:18.140
control.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:19.815
And basically
that's a very locked

00:08:19.815 --> 00:08:22.190
down-- think of its principle
of least privileged access.

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:25.280
So for people-- for
SELinux, it's basically

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.500
everything is broken down
into little components that

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:29.670
are domains, and if
something gets exploited

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:33.140
in one particular domain, it's
contained within that domain,

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:37.070
so it doesn't mean that the
whole system gets compromised.

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:39.440
And then moving further up,
obviously everything is--

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:40.520
a sandbox.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.620
Every application is a
sandboxed within Android

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:44.480
because it relies
on the Linux kernel.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:45.950
And what's a little bit
different between Android

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:48.280
and traditional Linux is
that every process actually

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:49.530
has its own unique identifier.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:50.947
So unlike Linux,
traditional Linux

00:08:50.947 --> 00:08:54.140
where every process is
tied to that specific Linux

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:55.640
user that's logged,
in this case,

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:57.640
every process is tied to--

00:08:57.640 --> 00:08:59.198
it has its own unique UID.

00:08:59.198 --> 00:09:01.490
And in this case, that's what
makes everything sandbox,

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:04.220
so every process has access
to its own resources,

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:05.563
its own memory, its own storage.

00:09:05.563 --> 00:09:06.980
And in order to
share information,

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.605
you have to use system features
to be able to share information

00:09:09.605 --> 00:09:10.643
across, right?

00:09:10.643 --> 00:09:11.810
So it's completely isolated.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:13.490
We take it a step further
with the work profile, which

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:14.820
I'm going to cover in
much more depth later,

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:15.530
so I'm just going
to really quick

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:18.110
is that the work profile is
actually a separate Linux user.

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:20.203
So imagine-- and I'll
show screenshots of this--

00:09:20.203 --> 00:09:22.620
who's here is familiar with
Android Enterprise real quick?

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:23.293
Anybody?

00:09:23.293 --> 00:09:23.913
Couple of you?

00:09:23.913 --> 00:09:24.710
Oh, that's really good.

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:26.377
So-- was it because
there was an earlier

00:09:26.377 --> 00:09:27.683
session you saw about this?

00:09:27.683 --> 00:09:29.975
Anybody-- was there another
Android Enterprise session?

00:09:29.975 --> 00:09:31.370
Oh, good to know.

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:32.870
So I'll cover it a
little bit later,

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:35.203
but the work profile is a
really elegant user experience

00:09:35.203 --> 00:09:37.610
where it's basically
a second Linux

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.815
user in the same
pane of glass, so

00:09:39.815 --> 00:09:41.690
that all the data that
is in the work profile

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:42.540
is completely separate.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:44.165
It's got its own set
of encryption keys

00:09:44.165 --> 00:09:46.190
that can be managed by IT.

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:48.830
They can be evicted when
you log out, and provides

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.247
that additional control, which
I'll dive into a little bit

00:09:51.247 --> 00:09:51.810
later.

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:54.537
So next up is anti-exploitation.

00:09:54.537 --> 00:09:56.120
This is like the
toughest slide for me

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:58.100
to cover because
it's so technical.

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:01.190
But basically, there's
a big misconception

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:05.900
about bugs and vulnerabilities
and how they're exploitable.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:08.333
Just because there's a bug,
obviously these operating

00:10:08.333 --> 00:10:10.250
systems are hundreds of
thousands and millions

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:13.142
lines of code, and so
it's inevitable there's

00:10:13.142 --> 00:10:13.850
going to be bugs.

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:16.017
It's inevitable that there
could be a vulnerability,

00:10:16.017 --> 00:10:19.310
but it doesn't mean that a bug
has to be exploitable, right?

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:21.282
It'd be turned into
an exploit that can

00:10:21.282 --> 00:10:22.490
compromise the system, right?

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:23.950
So over the years,
we've-- actually,

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:26.325
this is one of the key themes
that we've been adding year

00:10:26.325 --> 00:10:29.240
over year, is additional
anti-exploitation techniques.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:32.180
And so for example, everybody's
really with address space

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:33.950
layout randomization.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.720
We had that in the
user space for years.

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:38.550
We actually added that
in the kernel in Oreo.

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:40.550
Randomizing memory makes
it difficult to target

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:43.610
exploited code and
privileged components.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:45.350
We have hardened user
copy that prevents

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:47.360
overflow using system APIs.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:48.800
PAN emulation
provides the kernel

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:50.900
from accessing
user space memory.

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:54.380
And then when-- in Pie we add
a control flow integrity which

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:57.640
basically instrument runtime
operations to fail gracefully

00:10:57.640 --> 00:10:59.830
if some undefined
behavior occurs.

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:02.670
So again, the real thing is
that just because it's a bug

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:05.170
doesn't-- just because you have
a bug doesn't mean it can be

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:06.450
exploited, right?

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:09.000
And so again, we
talked about-- just

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.730
to quickly recap, the
hardware-based security

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:14.070
stuff above in terms
of the Android kernel

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:17.670
and everything there, and
then the anti-exploitation

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:18.170
techniques.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:19.710
So one of the things
that people always ask,

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:21.752
because one of the ways
we try to measure success

00:11:21.752 --> 00:11:24.780
is externally, right?

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:26.280
So one of the ways
we try to measure

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:28.590
this is through vulnerability
reward pricing, right?

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:30.820
That's kind of a supply
and demand situation.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:33.960
And so if you look at this,
this is basically pricing

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.700
of our vulnerability
rewards versus Apple's.

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:40.047
These are both publicly
available on both [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:40.047 --> 00:11:41.130
Like their site, our site.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.380
And what you'll see is that
they're pretty much on par

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:45.120
with Android
actually paying more

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:46.920
for certain categories
of exploits.

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:49.920
And so you can kind of imagine,
based on supply and demand,

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.440
if it's harder to do, it's
going to cost more money, right?

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:56.070
But again, this is our pricing,
so this is a little subjective.

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:59.220
So the next scenario then is,
what about third party pricing?

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:02.280
So one of the big research
competitions when it comes

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:04.920
to mobility is Mobile Pwn2Own.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:07.420
It's in Tokyo,
Japan, every year.

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:09.420
And what you'll see over
the last three years,

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:13.650
the pricing between
Google devices and iPhones

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:15.270
has steadily become equal.

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:18.030
And so in 2018 they were
actually completely equal.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:21.690
And what's interesting about
Pwn2Own, in the last two years,

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:25.170
the Pixel 2 was in
Pwn2Own 2017 and 2018,

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:28.463
it was not exploited in
either of those years.

00:12:28.463 --> 00:12:30.630
There were some Android
devices that were exploited,

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:33.430
but they were not using
vulnerabilities in Android Open

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:33.930
Source.

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:35.888
They basically had specific
custom applications

00:12:35.888 --> 00:12:38.120
that were basically exploited.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:40.050
It was nothing to do
with Android Open Source.

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:42.600
And in comparison,
the iPhone was

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.360
pwned, as the term is,
twice in competition

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:49.890
and once after time had expired.

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:53.130
So another piece in terms of
the system security, obviously

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:54.390
network security.

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:56.370
Way back when, I remember
doing security demos.

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:57.602
The easy one was the--

00:12:57.602 --> 00:12:59.685
you convince a user to
install a root certificate,

00:12:59.685 --> 00:13:02.560
self-signed root certificate,
and you run mitmproxy

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:04.860
and it's like the coolest
demo-- scares everybody away.

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:06.360
That's not a reality
anymore, right?

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:09.450
And so network security has
steadily been improving release

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:10.420
after release.

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:14.610
So in Pie, we mandated
TLS by default. Also,

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:16.710
app developers actually
have a lot of flexibility

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:18.543
in terms of like even
making it more secure.

00:13:18.543 --> 00:13:22.050
So you can do TLS leaf pinning
by simply modifying the network

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:25.300
security config in your
application, at which point

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:28.410
you can just pin
specific connections.

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:30.840
As of Android
Nougat/Android 7.0,

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:33.600
we added basically
restrictions where

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:36.450
all applications on the phone,
unless they explicitly opt out,

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:39.660
will only trust the
built-in root certificate

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:41.790
authorities that ship on
the device, which are then

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:43.198
updated by us over the year.

00:13:43.198 --> 00:13:45.240
So-- I mean, that's really
what negates the whole

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:49.148
mitmproxy or installing a
self-signed root certificate.

00:13:49.148 --> 00:13:50.940
And then from a privacy
perspective in Pie,

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:53.870
we actually added something we
call DNS over TLS, which is--

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:55.980
we were the first major
mobile OS to do this.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:59.098
And this basically takes
all your DNS traffic

00:13:59.098 --> 00:14:00.390
and encrypts it as well, right?

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:02.190
So somebody can't
see what request

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:04.830
you're making if you're on
some other Wi-Fi network,

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:06.920
for example, or the carrier.

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:09.870
And the last one is a
specific-- excuse me--

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:12.420
Pixel Nexus feature.

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:16.458
It's called Wi-Fi Assistant,
where basically it

00:14:16.458 --> 00:14:18.000
allows you to connect
to public Wi-Fi

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:21.600
and then launches a Google VPN
built in for you automatically.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.480
Of course, for people that don't
have Pixel or Nexus devices,

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:26.400
there's dozens of
VPN providers if you

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:29.483
wanted to do that, third
party ones in the Play Store.

00:14:29.483 --> 00:14:31.650
So the next thing is we
talk about all these amazing

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:33.330
features we keep
adding, but users

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:35.040
need to install these features.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.720
And so one of the things
we do, like I mentioned,

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:40.990
is we provide monthly security
bulletins to the ecosystem.

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:43.470
So, I mean, different OEMs
pick them up different times,

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:45.270
but in terms of
enterprise customers,

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:47.100
we have-- if nobody's familiar
with Android Enterprise,

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:49.700
it's recommended where those
customers are picking them up

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:52.530
at max at 90 days, and some
are doing it at monthly.

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:55.057
And so we provide
these security updates,

00:14:55.057 --> 00:14:57.390
they're completely separate
from feature updates, right?

00:14:57.390 --> 00:14:58.860
We do dessert releases
which are annual,

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:00.750
and those are all the lumpy--
big-- lots of features.

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:02.200
Security updates are
meant to be small

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:03.420
so they can go through
the system quickly,

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:06.000
people can vet them and they can
get installed on these devices.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:07.710
One thing that we
added in Android Nougat

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:09.618
was called Seamless
Updates or A/B Updates.

00:15:09.618 --> 00:15:11.160
And this is really
convenient feature

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:12.390
because users have
this tendency,

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:13.260
they don't want to run updates.

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:15.270
Who remembers like these
Windows updating and you

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.040
see this blue screen
and it's just updating,

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:18.300
updating, updating, updating.

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:20.520
So with Android Seamless,
it's similar to Chrome OS

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:21.900
with its seamless updates.

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:24.025
What happens is there's
two copies of the operating

00:15:24.025 --> 00:15:24.970
system on the device.

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:28.030
And so when you're running
the primary copy, the A copy,

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:31.518
it's downloading and installing
the B copy in the background

00:15:31.518 --> 00:15:32.560
while the user is idling.

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:34.435
So on a phone, it's
basically when the device

00:15:34.435 --> 00:15:38.540
is plugged into power and just
idling on Wi-Fi, no impact

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:39.180
to the user.

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:40.805
And then like you
see there, I actually

00:15:40.805 --> 00:15:43.938
took these screenshots, hence
it's so late in the evening.

00:15:43.938 --> 00:15:46.350
I was cramming
for this, I guess.

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:49.137
And so you can see that
the user gets prompted

00:15:49.137 --> 00:15:50.970
with a notification,
and all they have to do

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:52.928
is hit Restart and boom,
you're up and running.

00:15:52.928 --> 00:15:55.710
And the cool thing is,
when it reboots the device,

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:58.295
it basically verifies
that new copy.

00:15:58.295 --> 00:16:00.920
And if anything bad happens, it
simply reverts to the old copy.

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.450
No loss of user data, no impact
to the user, very elegant

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.130
experience.

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:07.890
So that was a Nougat and Oreo.

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:11.520
How many people are
familiar Project Treble?

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:12.442
One, two.

00:16:12.442 --> 00:16:13.150
OK, good to know.

00:16:13.150 --> 00:16:14.680
So Project Treble-- this
is actually really cool.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.010
So Project Treble is an
enhancement we made in Oreo.

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:19.140
It's mandatory on
all Oreo and devices

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:20.623
that ship with Oreo and newer.

00:16:20.623 --> 00:16:22.290
And basically it's a
further abstraction

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:24.450
between the firmware and the OS.

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:26.790
And this is actually
a feature that enables

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:28.467
OEMs to update much quicker.

00:16:28.467 --> 00:16:30.300
And so we've seen a lot
of progress to this.

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:31.950
So you can see on
the far right there,

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:33.330
there's some of the OEMs.

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:35.670
So like by the end of last
year, all of those OEMs

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:39.660
had devices updated to
Pie, from Oreo to Pie.

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:43.080
And we actually saw, compared
to the previous year from Nougat

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.930
to Oreo, a 3x
increase of devices

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:49.230
that updated from Nougat
to Oreo versus Oreo to Pie.

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:50.102
So a lot more--

00:16:50.102 --> 00:16:51.810
it's really so far
benefited quite a bit.

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:56.950
So kind of to--

00:16:56.950 --> 00:17:00.370
I thought I put my phone on Do
Not Disturb, it's so strange.

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:02.950
Sorry.

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:06.339
So anyways, Android patching has
improved as a result of Treble

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:08.470
and like the other
seamless updates.

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:10.842
Our partner programs, just
some of the quick stats,

00:17:10.842 --> 00:17:12.550
and this is in our
annual year in review.

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:15.250
Over 1 billion-plus devices
were patched in 2018.

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:20.470
More specifically in Q4,
almost 30% of devices more

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:23.920
were patched in Q4 of 2018
than the previous quarter.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:26.530
And year over year,
in Q4 of 2018,

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:29.480
we had 84% more devices patched
than in the same quarter

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:30.550
of the prior year.

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:32.260
So patching has
improved, which is really

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:34.620
good for the ecosystem, right?

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:35.840
So then focusing on apps.

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:38.282
Like I mentioned earlier,
there are two types--

00:17:38.282 --> 00:17:39.240
I look at it two types.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.960
There's faulty apps-- developers
are making accidental mistakes.

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:43.400
They're not trying to be
malicious with what they're

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:45.858
doing, but they're basically
running through-- they're just

00:17:45.858 --> 00:17:47.160
not experts in certain things.

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:49.460
And then there's the
malicious apps, right?

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:52.460
Malware, malicious apps trying
to exfiltrate data from users

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:53.990
and be annoying
with them, right?

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:56.390
So examples of
poorly-written apps

00:17:56.390 --> 00:17:59.120
is like with transport security,
improper certificate path

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:00.980
elevation, and so forth, right?

00:18:00.980 --> 00:18:03.170
So our way of handling
this, of course,

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:06.440
is you've heard people, process,
tools, this is my version--

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:08.210
policy, education,
and technology.

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:11.220
So in terms of policy,
obviously there's

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:13.220
the developer policy
guidelines that are online.

00:18:13.220 --> 00:18:14.210
Developers must follow these.

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:15.920
There's a lot about
security and abuse

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:17.600
and so forth that
they need to abide by.

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:19.940
From an education perspective,
we spent a lot of time

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:21.380
like notifying
developers of when

00:18:21.380 --> 00:18:23.213
we make policy changes,
what they need to do

00:18:23.213 --> 00:18:25.665
to abide by these changes.

00:18:25.665 --> 00:18:27.290
We provide tutorials
for these changes,

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:28.820
and I'm going to cover
a good example there.

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:31.280
And on the technology side,
think of it as the guardrails,

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:31.490
right?

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:33.830
So like we insure in case
they do mess something up

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:37.700
or something that's more
like the malicious scenario,

00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:41.067
we have the tools in place
to protect against that, OK?

00:18:41.067 --> 00:18:42.400
So here's a really good example.

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:46.662
This is actually from a blog we
launched in December of 2017.

00:18:46.662 --> 00:18:48.370
And this was something
where we announced

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:50.530
that we were mandating
that developers

00:18:50.530 --> 00:18:52.510
use Target API Level 26.

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:54.640
So basically in their
apps, they targeted--

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:57.690
with every release, when we
make security enhancements,

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.490
it's tied to a specific
dessert feature release.

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:02.170
And so there's an
API-level version.

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:04.270
So API Level 26 was Oreo.

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:09.850
And in this case, we said
that as of August of 2018,

00:19:09.850 --> 00:19:12.580
any new application that's being
submitted to the Play Store

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:19.150
must meet that target API level,
and in November, any updates

00:19:19.150 --> 00:19:20.990
must also meet that
target API level.

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:23.410
And so some examples of
where that improved ecosystem

00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:30.100
is, we added runtime permissions
in Lollipop, API Level 23.

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:32.770
We added the built-in using
only the default trustStore

00:19:32.770 --> 00:19:34.630
in API Level 24,
which is Nougat.

00:19:34.630 --> 00:19:38.530
And like I said, in Pie
we had TLS by default,

00:19:38.530 --> 00:19:39.610
which is API Level 28.

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:41.770
So we notified the developers.

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:44.027
We provided them
plenty of tutorials.

00:19:44.027 --> 00:19:46.360
We actually put this right
in the Developer Play Console

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:47.870
where when they would
submit an application,

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:50.412
they would see this kind of a
heads-up-- hey, this is coming,

00:19:50.412 --> 00:19:51.280
this is coming.

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:55.382
And the good news is that
this year, we had about--

00:19:55.382 --> 00:19:57.340
we had actually had pretty
much close to 100%--

00:19:57.340 --> 00:19:58.810
everybody, I mean,
complied, right?

00:19:58.810 --> 00:19:59.387
They had to.

00:19:59.387 --> 00:20:00.970
That was where the
technology came in.

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:02.638
If they didn't,
their new updates

00:20:02.638 --> 00:20:04.180
weren't going to
get approved, right?

00:20:04.180 --> 00:20:05.890
So what's really
cool about this now

00:20:05.890 --> 00:20:08.135
is that we've set
the standard, and now

00:20:08.135 --> 00:20:09.760
we just had a blog
come out in February

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:12.452
of this year doing the same
thing now with API Level 28.

00:20:12.452 --> 00:20:13.952
So every year we're
going n minus 1,

00:20:13.952 --> 00:20:18.610
so right now, the same time
this year, August and November,

00:20:18.610 --> 00:20:20.740
they have to update
to API Level 28, which

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:23.320
now will mandate new features
that they have to apply, right?

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:26.830
That will apply to
their applications.

00:20:26.830 --> 00:20:29.238
So another really neat
program, and this is the--

00:20:29.238 --> 00:20:30.780
back to the technology
aspect of this

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:32.655
is the Play App Security
Improvement Program.

00:20:32.655 --> 00:20:34.520
Has anybody heard
of this program?

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:36.478
When I joined, this was
like the coolest thing.

00:20:36.478 --> 00:20:40.417
So what this does is-- this
is basically started in 2014.

00:20:40.417 --> 00:20:42.000
And again, this is
for developers just

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:42.708
to make mistakes.

00:20:42.708 --> 00:20:45.320
They might be using the wrong
version of OpenSSL that might

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:46.540
have a known vulnerability.

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:49.550
Their app might be susceptible
cross-site scripting.

00:20:49.550 --> 00:20:51.570
They're not doing proper
certificate validation.

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:53.778
There's actually 31 total
classes of vulnerabilities,

00:20:53.778 --> 00:20:55.603
and we added, I
think, eight in 2018.

00:20:55.603 --> 00:20:58.020
And what this does is when
they submit the app to the Play

00:20:58.020 --> 00:20:59.910
Developer Console, it's like
a free scan that they get.

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:01.327
And so when this
app gets scanned,

00:21:01.327 --> 00:21:03.243
when their app is
submitted, they get scanned.

00:21:03.243 --> 00:21:04.940
If there's an issue
that it gets found,

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:06.680
it pops up an alert
in the console,

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:09.110
it gives them a tutorial
on how to fix that issue,

00:21:09.110 --> 00:21:10.068
it sends them an email.

00:21:10.068 --> 00:21:11.902
And the really neat
thing about this program

00:21:11.902 --> 00:21:14.240
is as you can see to date
since we launched the program,

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:16.310
we've helped over
300,000 app developers

00:21:16.310 --> 00:21:17.710
fix over a million applications.

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:19.460
So it's something they
just get for free--

00:21:19.460 --> 00:21:21.275
like a free assessment,
security assessment

00:21:21.275 --> 00:21:22.150
of their application.

00:21:22.150 --> 00:21:23.630
And again, it's not for
malicious applications,

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:25.713
it's just for developers
that are making mistakes.

00:21:27.725 --> 00:21:30.100
And then the last thing, just
like with seamless updates,

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:32.350
just to cover a super
quick, is the Play

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:35.470
Store also does auto updates
and it does this by default.

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:36.970
App developers also
release security

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:38.410
fixes to their applications.

00:21:38.410 --> 00:21:40.450
And just like seamless
updates for the device,

00:21:40.450 --> 00:21:42.430
if the device is powered and
connected to Wi-Fi and idle,

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:44.170
these updates run
in the background.

00:21:44.170 --> 00:21:46.600
No user intervention required.

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:48.250
And it's really
meant to just ensure

00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:49.210
that they're always
running the latest

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:50.350
version of the application.

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:52.300
Obviously there's some
benefit to the user experience

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:53.620
because they always
add new features.

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:55.190
There's also a huge benefit
from the security side

00:21:55.190 --> 00:21:57.580
because they're getting
the latest and greatest

00:21:57.580 --> 00:22:00.730
on the security side as well.

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:04.140
So next up is the
malware aspect.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:05.860
And so obviously you
heard in the news,

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:08.840
we know that there's articles
about Android and malware,

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:11.130
but malware is a universal risk.

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:14.520
It doesn't just impact Android,
it impacts every operating

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:15.490
system out there.

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:18.780
And so just a couple
of example headlines.

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:21.222
I mean, it even impacts
the iPhone and iOS, right?

00:22:21.222 --> 00:22:22.680
There was actually
a recent article

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:25.180
that came out this week about
a new malware variant for iOS.

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:27.500
And there was examples
of using profiles,

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:30.305
using side-loading apps for
enterprise app developer

00:22:30.305 --> 00:22:32.180
certificates, there's--
like I said, malware,

00:22:32.180 --> 00:22:33.670
I'm not poking at
one or the other,

00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:36.750
we all know that there's
a malware risk out there.

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:39.900
And so how do you
protect against malware?

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:42.460
Our philosophy is that malware
protection should be built in.

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:44.678
So if you look at Windows,
they have Defender.

00:22:44.678 --> 00:22:47.220
If you look at Chrome and Chrome
OS, they have Safe Browsing.

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:49.720
And if you look at Android,
we have Google Play Protect.

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:51.803
How many people here are
familiar with Google Play

00:22:51.803 --> 00:22:52.930
Protect?

00:22:52.930 --> 00:22:54.660
Ooh, that's a good number.

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:55.250
Cool.

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:57.520
So for those that aren't--

00:22:57.520 --> 00:22:59.590
work with me,
clicker-- there we go.

00:22:59.590 --> 00:23:02.290
Google Play Protect is the
world's most widely-used

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:04.120
anti-malware solution out there.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:07.210
So it basically scans
over 50 billion apps

00:23:07.210 --> 00:23:10.150
across 2 billion devices
every single day.

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:11.168
It's actually a client--

00:23:11.168 --> 00:23:13.210
it's a client application,
but it's a whole cloud

00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:14.450
infrastructure as well.

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:17.740
And on the back end we actually
verify 500,000 applications

00:23:17.740 --> 00:23:19.180
every single day, OK?

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:21.670
And so Play Protect
uses the combination

00:23:21.670 --> 00:23:25.510
of machine learning, static
analysis, dynamic analysis,

00:23:25.510 --> 00:23:29.500
heuristics, signature
checks, heat maps,

00:23:29.500 --> 00:23:31.240
and a lot of other
techniques to identify

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:33.340
what we call Potentially
Harmful Applications--

00:23:33.340 --> 00:23:36.460
PHAs for short-- which is
our terminology for malware.

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:39.370
And it does this for both
on Play and off Play,

00:23:39.370 --> 00:23:41.020
because there are
users out there

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:43.522
in certain regions that like
to download applications,

00:23:43.522 --> 00:23:44.980
side load applications
that are not

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:46.150
available on the Play Store.

00:23:46.150 --> 00:23:48.880
And so Google Play Protect
covers those users as well.

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:51.970
And a really good example
from our past year in review

00:23:51.970 --> 00:23:55.690
is that we had prevented 1.6
billion installation attempts

00:23:55.690 --> 00:23:58.330
of potentially harmful
applications for off of Play

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:00.130
applications last
year alone, right?

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:03.592
In 2018 alone.

00:24:03.592 --> 00:24:05.800
So again, going back to the
whole measurable security

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:10.308
piece, in 2018 we launched--

00:24:10.308 --> 00:24:12.600
rather, two weeks ago, we
published our year in review,

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:15.020
we do this annual recap, and
one of the charts we showed

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:17.310
was PHA install rates over time.

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:20.720
And what you'll see is, the
install rate for on Play

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:23.270
has been very low, and
what's interesting is,

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:25.140
it kind of looks
like it's stagnating,

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:26.150
but the reality
is we're actually

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:27.590
adding more categories
every year--

00:24:27.590 --> 00:24:29.507
we're adding different
variants that we didn't

00:24:29.507 --> 00:24:30.690
think about the year before.

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:31.850
So it's kind of an
unfair scenario,

00:24:31.850 --> 00:24:34.040
because if we showed only
the ones that we tracked year

00:24:34.040 --> 00:24:35.570
after year that were the
same, it would actually

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:36.870
continuously go down.

00:24:36.870 --> 00:24:38.210
But the fact that we keep
adding more and more,

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:39.877
it kind of provides
this view that we're

00:24:39.877 --> 00:24:42.937
kind of going still
or consistent across.

00:24:42.937 --> 00:24:45.020
But you can see off Play,
that number continuously

00:24:45.020 --> 00:24:45.687
still goes down.

00:24:45.687 --> 00:24:47.687
And so every year we have
this like-- we come up

00:24:47.687 --> 00:24:48.800
with the nifty analogy.

00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:50.883
They're usually really
morbid even before my time.

00:24:50.883 --> 00:24:52.640
I don't know why they
came up with like--

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:54.950
two years ago it was like
the likelihood of getting hit

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:57.878
by a meteor, it was higher than
installing malware from PHA--

00:24:57.878 --> 00:24:59.420
malware from the
Play Store last year

00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:01.100
was like struck by lightning.

00:25:01.100 --> 00:25:03.020
This year it's, you have
a higher probability

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:04.910
of dying in a bathtub
than installing

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:08.420
a potentially harmful
application on your device.

00:25:08.420 --> 00:25:08.920
All right.

00:25:08.920 --> 00:25:11.590
So next, phishing.

00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:13.785
Phishing is pretty obvious.

00:25:13.785 --> 00:25:15.160
Even though in
the last section I

00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:17.930
talked about how malware
is a universal risk

00:25:17.930 --> 00:25:20.540
and continued to rise, phishing
actually overtook malware.

00:25:20.540 --> 00:25:22.850
So we, again, back to
the transparency reports,

00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:26.270
the Safe Browsing team publishes
their own transparency report,

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:31.220
and what they showed is in
2016, phishing overtook malware

00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:35.158
as the bigger attack
surface, and that has

00:25:35.158 --> 00:25:36.450
continued through today, right?

00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:38.960
So that trend has continued
since then-- since 2016.

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:41.037
And when you think of
phishing, obviously

00:25:41.037 --> 00:25:42.620
trying to compromise
user credentials,

00:25:42.620 --> 00:25:47.155
user info through email,
messages, SMS, any forms of--

00:25:47.155 --> 00:25:48.530
where you can fool
a user, right?

00:25:48.530 --> 00:25:51.072
And actually, an interesting
one that has cropped up recently

00:25:51.072 --> 00:25:53.130
is voice-related phishing, OK?

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:55.152
So I'm not saying
we have the end all,

00:25:55.152 --> 00:25:56.860
be all solutions to
protect against this,

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:58.940
but what I am saying is we have
a lot of built-in solutions

00:25:58.940 --> 00:26:00.560
that users don't
know about, right?

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:03.330
And so Safe Browsing is a
really good example of that.

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:05.900
How many people are
familiar with Safe Browsing?

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:06.660
Everybody, yeah.

00:26:06.660 --> 00:26:07.327
So this is cool.

00:26:07.327 --> 00:26:09.170
So Safe Browsing
launched in 2007

00:26:09.170 --> 00:26:10.970
to protect against
phishing and malware.

00:26:10.970 --> 00:26:13.070
And it's built into
the Chrome browser,

00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:15.320
so it's available across
every single operating system

00:26:15.320 --> 00:26:16.760
out there that runs Chrome.

00:26:16.760 --> 00:26:19.160
So that means we have 3
billion-plus devices protected,

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:22.250
and it examines billions of URLs
and continuously re-examines

00:26:22.250 --> 00:26:23.150
them, right?

00:26:23.150 --> 00:26:25.150
There's actually an API
as well that they offer.

00:26:25.150 --> 00:26:27.620
So any third-party
application or browser

00:26:27.620 --> 00:26:28.880
can leverage Safe Browsing.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:31.505
And so when you think, what does
that have to do with phishing?

00:26:31.505 --> 00:26:34.100
Well, on an Android device
where Chrome OS is your default

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:36.972
browser, you launch a link
from your text messaging

00:26:36.972 --> 00:26:38.930
application, you launch
a link from your email,

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:40.347
you launch the
link from anywhere,

00:26:40.347 --> 00:26:43.970
if it opens up with Chrome or
like a custom tab with Chrome,

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:47.240
you will see this pop up, OK?

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:49.760
So it does cover some of that.

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:54.000
And then when we talk about
additional phishing protection,

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:54.500
right?

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:57.800
So there's-- against phishing,
a lot of people talk about

00:26:57.800 --> 00:27:01.330
step-up verification or
two-factor authentication.

00:27:01.330 --> 00:27:04.420
So-- deep breath--

00:27:04.420 --> 00:27:06.720
sorry, I get too excited.

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:08.465
There's still a flaw
with most versions

00:27:08.465 --> 00:27:10.090
of two-factor
authentication out there.

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:13.140
So if you do like SMS-based
or phone-based or text or

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:16.140
OTP-based, you could still
provide that information

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:17.910
to whoever trying
to phish you, right?

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:21.270
So with hardware-backed keys,
though-- hardware-based keys,

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:21.922
rather--

00:27:21.922 --> 00:27:23.880
it actually specifically
protects against that.

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:27.623
So like FIDO U2F, they actually
do a check to make sure

00:27:27.623 --> 00:27:29.040
that you're hitting
the right URL,

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:30.780
and it's one of the
only ways to actually

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:32.270
truly protect against phishing.

00:27:32.270 --> 00:27:34.770
And even internally, Google, I
think we talked about it last

00:27:34.770 --> 00:27:37.350
year where since we've
implemented hardware-based keys

00:27:37.350 --> 00:27:38.760
at Google, we have--

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:41.413
not a single employee has been
phished that we know of, right?

00:27:41.413 --> 00:27:42.830
What's really
cool-- and I'm going

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:45.670
to spend a couple of slides on
this because it's the inverse,

00:27:45.670 --> 00:27:47.500
we made a big
announcement yesterday.

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:49.750
And so the people that raised
their hand and said they

00:27:49.750 --> 00:27:53.160
have an Android phone, we now
can leverage these Android

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:56.760
devices as actual
hardware security keys,

00:27:56.760 --> 00:28:01.180
FIDO U2F keys to authenticate
with your laptop or desktop.

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:03.600
So now the phone
becomes the key, right?

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:06.433
And so this actually works
on Android Nougat and above.

00:28:06.433 --> 00:28:08.100
So that's over a
billion Android devices

00:28:08.100 --> 00:28:10.140
that can take advantage
of this functionality.

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:12.307
And it actually uses, again,
hardware-based security

00:28:12.307 --> 00:28:15.835
because it's using that
trusted execution environment

00:28:15.835 --> 00:28:17.460
to generate and
protect those keys that

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:19.620
are used in conjunction.

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:22.050
And then on the Pixel 3
specifically, it actually

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:24.810
uses that discrete hardware.

00:28:24.810 --> 00:28:28.770
So just to give you an example
what that user experience looks

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:30.032
like.

00:28:30.032 --> 00:28:32.490
You'll see when you try to log
onto your desktop or laptop,

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:36.210
to G Suite or to Gmail
or whatever, you'll

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:37.990
see a pop-up on your phone.

00:28:37.990 --> 00:28:39.990
This is on a device that isn't--

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:41.640
this is not a Pixel
3 device example.

00:28:41.640 --> 00:28:43.140
And you'll simply
just authenticate.

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:44.730
You hit Yes and
you're good to go.

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:48.030
On a Pixel 3 specifically
like I mentioned,

00:28:48.030 --> 00:28:51.570
it leverages a discrete
tamper-resistant piece

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:53.250
of hardware called
the Titan M chip.

00:28:53.250 --> 00:28:55.167
It's basically analogous
to a secure element--

00:28:55.167 --> 00:28:58.500
it's a separate chip, hence
the really nice drawing there.

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:01.890
And the way that works is--
so the keys there are actually

00:29:01.890 --> 00:29:04.050
stored in that
chip, and you have

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:06.780
to hard-press the Volume
Down button to prove intent

00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:09.850
and that you're a person
present to authenticate.

00:29:09.850 --> 00:29:11.820
So it provides a higher
level of assurance,

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:15.487
and again, these
chips are much more--

00:29:15.487 --> 00:29:17.820
they protect much better
against hardware-based attacks.

00:29:20.615 --> 00:29:21.990
Last one I wanted
to cover really

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:23.550
quickly was voice phishing
because I feel like this is

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:25.290
the one that's most annoying.

00:29:25.290 --> 00:29:27.568
And again, not covering
every single example here.

00:29:27.568 --> 00:29:29.610
There's obviously probably
sophisticated examples

00:29:29.610 --> 00:29:31.318
that this wouldn't
cover, but this covers

00:29:31.318 --> 00:29:32.700
some basic examples, right?

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:35.250
So according to First
Orion, almost half

00:29:35.250 --> 00:29:38.310
of US cell phone calls will
be scams by the next year.

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:40.270
I'm sure everybody
here feels that pain.

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:41.920
I get a lot of these calls.

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:44.998
I didn't have to
mock up screenshots,

00:29:44.998 --> 00:29:47.040
these are actual real
shots I took from my phone.

00:29:47.040 --> 00:29:49.332
Well, the second one is not
because it's a fake number.

00:29:49.332 --> 00:29:50.890
But like this
happens all the time.

00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:53.670
So on Android Nougat
and above, again,

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:56.310
a billion-plus devices, if
you're using the default phone

00:29:56.310 --> 00:29:58.050
application provided
by Google, this

00:29:58.050 --> 00:30:01.200
is actually a built-in feature,
the caller ID spam protection.

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:03.197
So on other operating
systems that's something

00:30:03.197 --> 00:30:05.280
you have to buy from a
third party or the carrier.

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:07.110
On Android, this is something
that you get for free, right?

00:30:07.110 --> 00:30:08.235
This is a built-in feature.

00:30:08.235 --> 00:30:10.410
You can actually say,
hey, if it's a spam call,

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:11.880
just don't even let it show up.

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:14.437
And the call screening is
a Pixel-exclusive feature

00:30:14.437 --> 00:30:16.770
that launched with the Pixel
3, but it's actually become

00:30:16.770 --> 00:30:18.150
my favorite feature by far.

00:30:18.150 --> 00:30:22.560
Because if you really
want to annoy people

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:24.140
that try to phish
for information,

00:30:24.140 --> 00:30:26.640
just turn on the call screening
and let them deal with that.

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:27.600
This is the coolest feature.

00:30:27.600 --> 00:30:28.560
I use this 10 times a day.

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:29.700
The biggest mistake
I ever made, though,

00:30:29.700 --> 00:30:31.200
was using it against my wife.

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:32.550
She still hasn't
forgiven me about that,

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:33.600
she brings it up all the time.

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:35.100
But it's actually
my coolest feature.

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:36.558
And just for the
fun of it, they've

00:30:36.558 --> 00:30:38.190
added like saving
your transcript

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:39.510
so then you can read it later.

00:30:39.510 --> 00:30:42.550
It's actually pretty amusing.

00:30:42.550 --> 00:30:43.050
All right.

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:49.640
So now covering the end user,
the weakest link, the biggest

00:30:49.640 --> 00:30:50.390
risk.

00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:53.407
I had this really funny
definition of end user,

00:30:53.407 --> 00:30:55.490
but they made me take it
down because it was mean,

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:56.900
but I'm going to say it anyways.

00:30:56.900 --> 00:30:59.060
It was end user,
which is a noun,

00:30:59.060 --> 00:31:01.190
and it's the term
IT professionals use

00:31:01.190 --> 00:31:02.300
when they mean idiot.

00:31:02.300 --> 00:31:03.807
So there, I've said it.

00:31:03.807 --> 00:31:06.140
I took it off the slides but
I wanted to say it anyways.

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:07.337
It's pretty funny.

00:31:07.337 --> 00:31:08.670
But again, I don't believe that.

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:09.770
I think end users
can be educated,

00:31:09.770 --> 00:31:11.853
and they just don't
appreciate the risk they often

00:31:11.853 --> 00:31:14.210
inflict on themselves and
the enterprise, right?

00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:17.690
So just quick a couple
examples that I put together.

00:31:17.690 --> 00:31:20.750
Obviously end users
sometimes don't

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:22.500
want to pay for
applications from the Play

00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:24.208
Store or the iTunes
App Store, so they're

00:31:24.208 --> 00:31:25.850
going to try to
side-load applications.

00:31:25.850 --> 00:31:29.840
This is possible on iOS, this
is possible on Android, right?

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:33.710
End users tend to be really
finicky about not using

00:31:33.710 --> 00:31:36.500
the IT-provided applications
for collaboration or content

00:31:36.500 --> 00:31:37.698
creation or whatever.

00:31:37.698 --> 00:31:38.990
So they're like, you know what?

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:40.190
I'll just use my
own applications

00:31:40.190 --> 00:31:42.565
and make my life easier because
that's what I like to use

00:31:42.565 --> 00:31:43.640
and I'm stubborn.

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:46.070
And of course, end users try
to root devices or jailbreak

00:31:46.070 --> 00:31:48.530
their devices because they
want to provide their own look

00:31:48.530 --> 00:31:50.390
and feel or customization
that they maybe

00:31:50.390 --> 00:31:52.182
aren't happy with the
stock version, right?

00:31:52.182 --> 00:31:54.307
And so an interesting survey
from a couple of years

00:31:54.307 --> 00:31:56.540
ago says nearly three out
of four government agencies

00:31:56.540 --> 00:31:58.630
experienced a security
issue last year,

00:31:58.630 --> 00:32:02.464
and 100% cite employees as their
largest security threat, OK?

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:07.690
So, since most people know
about Android Enterprise,

00:32:07.690 --> 00:32:09.422
I think that's here
to help, right?

00:32:09.422 --> 00:32:10.880
Android Enterprise
is here to help.

00:32:10.880 --> 00:32:15.400
So obviously Android has
had Enterprise functionality

00:32:15.400 --> 00:32:18.130
for many years,
going back to Froyo.

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:20.650
Early as Froyo you
could do basic stuff

00:32:20.650 --> 00:32:24.070
with device administrator
APIs, but since then it's

00:32:24.070 --> 00:32:26.555
evolved to something new
with Android Enterprise,

00:32:26.555 --> 00:32:28.180
and we have 80-plus
management settings

00:32:28.180 --> 00:32:30.040
that Enterprise
mobility manager vendors

00:32:30.040 --> 00:32:32.290
or unified endpoint manager
vendors can take advantage

00:32:32.290 --> 00:32:34.193
of and lock down these devices.

00:32:34.193 --> 00:32:35.860
And what's nice about
Android Enterprise

00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:38.740
is that it provides
nice data separation

00:32:38.740 --> 00:32:41.420
from personal and work and it
provides multiple management

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:41.920
modes.

00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:43.610
So let's take a look
at what that means.

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:45.525
So-- well first I
guess before that,

00:32:45.525 --> 00:32:47.650
if you look at the evolution
of Android Enterprise,

00:32:47.650 --> 00:32:49.465
it really started
with Lollipop where--

00:32:49.465 --> 00:32:51.340
that's when we came up
with the work profile,

00:32:51.340 --> 00:32:53.110
and kind of created
this paradigm shift.

00:32:53.110 --> 00:32:54.680
And I'm going to cover
that in the next slide.

00:32:54.680 --> 00:32:56.763
But you can see, we've
been steadily improving it.

00:32:56.763 --> 00:32:59.482
So like in Marshmallow, we
added additional key sharing

00:32:59.482 --> 00:33:00.940
functionality so
keys can be shared

00:33:00.940 --> 00:33:02.733
across Enterprise applications.

00:33:02.733 --> 00:33:04.900
So you don't have to like
share the same key somehow

00:33:04.900 --> 00:33:06.900
across 15 different
applications on a device,

00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:09.370
it could be centrally stored
and only work applications

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:11.360
can get access to it.

00:33:11.360 --> 00:33:13.720
We added a work-specific
security challenge in Nougat.

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:14.890
We added zero touch
enrollment, which

00:33:14.890 --> 00:33:16.098
I'll cover in the next slide.

00:33:16.098 --> 00:33:18.490
And then we added--

00:33:18.490 --> 00:33:21.100
in Pie, we refreshed
the user experience

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:23.795
to make it much more
elegant for end users.

00:33:23.795 --> 00:33:26.170
So first and foremost, for
users to set up these devices,

00:33:26.170 --> 00:33:26.800
times have changed.

00:33:26.800 --> 00:33:28.300
When you had a BlackBerry
back in the day,

00:33:28.300 --> 00:33:30.133
you kind of expected
IT to set up the device

00:33:30.133 --> 00:33:31.600
and they'd hand it
off to the user.

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:34.165
Nowadays, users are
distributed across everywhere.

00:33:34.165 --> 00:33:36.040
You mail that device to
the user and you just

00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:38.050
pray that they can
follow the instructions.

00:33:38.050 --> 00:33:40.990
So with Android Enterprise we
have quite a few different ways

00:33:40.990 --> 00:33:42.410
to set up these devices.

00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:46.240
So you can do a QR code, NFC
bump, short code identifier,

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:47.830
or zero touch,
which is basically--

00:33:47.830 --> 00:33:48.930
how many people-- sorry
for all the survey

00:33:48.930 --> 00:33:51.263
questions-- how many people
are familiar with zero touch

00:33:51.263 --> 00:33:52.050
enrollment?

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:52.550
Cool.

00:33:52.550 --> 00:33:55.300
OK, so zero touch is basically
analogous to Apple's Device

00:33:55.300 --> 00:33:56.430
Enrollment program.

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:59.755
That zero to 60 fully
automated, the user

00:33:59.755 --> 00:34:01.330
has to do nothing
basically, right?

00:34:01.330 --> 00:34:03.610
But even with the other
options, it's pretty quick.

00:34:03.610 --> 00:34:05.440
You scan whatever, you
enter your details,

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:06.440
and you're fully set up.

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:09.531
There's not much the user
has to do to deal with this.

00:34:09.531 --> 00:34:11.489
And then when it comes
to the management modes,

00:34:11.489 --> 00:34:14.409
so like I mentioned
earlier, Android Enterprise

00:34:14.409 --> 00:34:16.610
provides quite a
bit of flexibility.

00:34:16.610 --> 00:34:18.670
There's the work
profile for BYOD.

00:34:18.670 --> 00:34:21.219
And this is like a big
game changer, because if--

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:23.866
prior to here I was at
VMware working with AirWatch.

00:34:23.866 --> 00:34:26.199
Prior to that I was at a
startup called Good Technology.

00:34:26.199 --> 00:34:27.681
So I come from the EMM space.

00:34:27.681 --> 00:34:29.139
And at Good
Technology, we actually

00:34:29.139 --> 00:34:30.802
created what we
call the container.

00:34:30.802 --> 00:34:33.260
And the container was basically
the solution where we said,

00:34:33.260 --> 00:34:33.590
you know what?

00:34:33.590 --> 00:34:35.500
We're going to provide
managed applications where

00:34:35.500 --> 00:34:36.969
you don't manage
a device, but you

00:34:36.969 --> 00:34:38.889
can manage your own BYOD
applications, right?

00:34:38.889 --> 00:34:40.239
These line of business apps.

00:34:40.239 --> 00:34:42.429
And that was really good at the
time because it solved the gap,

00:34:42.429 --> 00:34:44.652
but if you look at the way
Android Enterprise handles

00:34:44.652 --> 00:34:47.110
the work profile, it removes
the need for these containers,

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:48.610
because containers
were proprietary.

00:34:48.610 --> 00:34:51.293
These were like-- every
vendor has their own SDK,

00:34:51.293 --> 00:34:53.210
and they have their own
line of business apps.

00:34:53.210 --> 00:34:54.793
So if you move from
one EMM to another

00:34:54.793 --> 00:34:56.168
and you try to do
the same model,

00:34:56.168 --> 00:34:58.251
you basically have to
switch all your apps around,

00:34:58.251 --> 00:34:59.680
they don't all work the same.

00:34:59.680 --> 00:35:01.555
With Android Enterprise
and the work profile,

00:35:01.555 --> 00:35:03.305
the work profile is
that container, right?

00:35:03.305 --> 00:35:05.380
It basically segregates
all your enterprise data.

00:35:05.380 --> 00:35:06.878
You can distribute
apps to that--

00:35:06.878 --> 00:35:08.920
that are commercially off
the shelf from the Play

00:35:08.920 --> 00:35:10.490
Store or your enterprise apps.

00:35:10.490 --> 00:35:14.020
They don't have to be wrapped
or customized in any way, right?

00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:16.630
So it doesn't matter
which EMM vendor you use,

00:35:16.630 --> 00:35:19.460
you kind of lift and shift, to
steal, I guess, a cloud term.

00:35:19.460 --> 00:35:21.713
And you can switch
vendors easily

00:35:21.713 --> 00:35:24.130
without any friction or impact
to your environment, right?

00:35:24.130 --> 00:35:26.310
In terms of device management.

00:35:26.310 --> 00:35:27.768
And then on the
company-owned side,

00:35:27.768 --> 00:35:30.018
obviously there's a lot of
different options you have.

00:35:30.018 --> 00:35:31.477
You can do a
fully-managed device

00:35:31.477 --> 00:35:34.060
where you don't let them put any
personal data on that device,

00:35:34.060 --> 00:35:35.810
or you can do a-- fully
manage with a work

00:35:35.810 --> 00:35:37.227
profile where you
still give them

00:35:37.227 --> 00:35:38.810
access to personal
data on the device,

00:35:38.810 --> 00:35:40.240
but it's a fully-managed
device, and you still

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:41.330
segregate your work data.

00:35:41.330 --> 00:35:44.410
So that's a really good example
for heavily regulated customers

00:35:44.410 --> 00:35:46.020
that are really
security-conscious.

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:48.520
And then you could do dedicated
devices for rugged or retail

00:35:48.520 --> 00:35:51.160
or warehouse scenarios, right?

00:35:51.160 --> 00:35:52.030
So in terms of--

00:35:54.670 --> 00:35:55.710
it benefits both, right?

00:35:55.710 --> 00:35:57.085
So I think I
covered this before,

00:35:57.085 --> 00:35:58.537
but in terms of
the work profile,

00:35:58.537 --> 00:36:00.245
it's a separate Linux
user on the device.

00:36:00.245 --> 00:36:02.590
So like, you're a personal
user, which is like user 0.

00:36:02.590 --> 00:36:05.650
The Linux user-- the second
work profile user is user 10.

00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:06.680
They're both available.

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:08.880
So you can see on
the screen there--

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:10.180
I wish I had a laser pointer.

00:36:10.180 --> 00:36:12.370
You can see on the screen
there that on one side

00:36:12.370 --> 00:36:14.170
you have personal, on the
other side you have work,

00:36:14.170 --> 00:36:16.030
but it's not like a desktop
where you log out of one

00:36:16.030 --> 00:36:17.200
and log into the other.

00:36:17.200 --> 00:36:18.370
They're available
at the same time--

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:19.960
this is just the
application drawer,

00:36:19.960 --> 00:36:23.007
but you can pin these on your
screen, so wherever they are--

00:36:23.007 --> 00:36:24.340
the only difference is you see--

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:26.170
there are some with a lock,
and the others are not.

00:36:26.170 --> 00:36:27.670
And you actually
can have the same--

00:36:27.670 --> 00:36:30.532
you have two Chromes, you can
have two Gmail or whatever

00:36:30.532 --> 00:36:31.240
clients you want.

00:36:31.240 --> 00:36:32.782
One is for work,
one is for personal,

00:36:32.782 --> 00:36:35.860
they're completely segregated
from one another, right?

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:37.390
And the real nice
benefit is you can

00:36:37.390 --> 00:36:39.848
do whatever you want in that
work profile, fully manage it.

00:36:39.848 --> 00:36:41.518
You can do VPN just
for those work apps.

00:36:41.518 --> 00:36:43.810
You can do whatever restrictions
and controls you want.

00:36:43.810 --> 00:36:46.120
Or, again, you can
manage the entire device

00:36:46.120 --> 00:36:47.650
and lock down
everything you would

00:36:47.650 --> 00:36:49.060
like you used to do
with a BlackBerry

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:50.935
or you do on an iPhone
where you can restrict

00:36:50.935 --> 00:36:53.997
access to camera,
what keyboard you use,

00:36:53.997 --> 00:36:56.080
don't let them modify Wi-Fi
and so forth-- there's

00:36:56.080 --> 00:36:57.247
80-plus management policies.

00:36:57.247 --> 00:36:59.705
But the real cool thing is it
benefits the end user, right?

00:36:59.705 --> 00:37:01.450
So the nice thing
about the work profile

00:37:01.450 --> 00:37:03.825
is it gives them peace of mind.

00:37:03.825 --> 00:37:05.200
Again, coming from
the EMM space,

00:37:05.200 --> 00:37:06.760
the thing that bothered
me the most is like, when

00:37:06.760 --> 00:37:08.927
you install a device
management profile and it says,

00:37:08.927 --> 00:37:09.980
you can trust us.

00:37:09.980 --> 00:37:13.612
We won't scan, inventory all
your personal apps, it's OK.

00:37:13.612 --> 00:37:15.070
Yes, we have the
permission, but we

00:37:15.070 --> 00:37:16.862
promise we're not going
to do that for you.

00:37:16.862 --> 00:37:18.822
With the work profile,
it's just not possible.

00:37:18.822 --> 00:37:21.280
The user knows what they're
getting into, and IT knows what

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:23.613
they're getting into because
there's comfortable balance

00:37:23.613 --> 00:37:27.280
there that IT is comfortable
that nothing will be able

00:37:27.280 --> 00:37:30.050
to get to this work data, and
the user knows that IT cannot

00:37:30.050 --> 00:37:30.550
overreach.

00:37:30.550 --> 00:37:32.610
The only things that IT
can do in this scenario

00:37:32.610 --> 00:37:35.800
when it's just work profile
is they can basically

00:37:35.800 --> 00:37:38.950
force the device passcode, and
like for example, as reaching

00:37:38.950 --> 00:37:42.657
outside of the work profile,
and now they can basically--

00:37:42.657 --> 00:37:44.990
as of Q, which is going to
covered in the other session,

00:37:44.990 --> 00:37:47.440
for example, you can ensure that
unknown sources is turned off

00:37:47.440 --> 00:37:49.000
on the personal side
so they can't side-load

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:50.080
any applications at all.

00:37:50.080 --> 00:37:52.840
That's the only overreach right
now on the work profile, right?

00:37:52.840 --> 00:37:54.340
So like outside of
the work profile.

00:37:54.340 --> 00:37:56.050
So it provides that
perfect balance.

00:37:56.050 --> 00:37:58.930
And actually, that
little toggle, people

00:37:58.930 --> 00:38:01.170
always think about turning
off their work profile.

00:38:01.170 --> 00:38:02.980
Yeah, I'm on vacation, I'm
going to turn this thing off

00:38:02.980 --> 00:38:04.660
and I don't think about
email or whatever.

00:38:04.660 --> 00:38:06.370
I think of it actually as a
really cool security feature,

00:38:06.370 --> 00:38:08.440
because I give my phone
to my kids all the time.

00:38:08.440 --> 00:38:09.580
And the worst thing
that can ever happen

00:38:09.580 --> 00:38:11.997
is my kid launches an email
client and responds to my boss

00:38:11.997 --> 00:38:12.790
with gibberish.

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:14.860
And what am I going to--

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:18.050
I've been breached,
I've been breached.

00:38:18.050 --> 00:38:22.428
So in terms of the next
piece, so we talked

00:38:22.428 --> 00:38:24.970
about the separation of data,
but how do you get applications

00:38:24.970 --> 00:38:25.540
in there?

00:38:25.540 --> 00:38:28.273
So we offer something
called Managed Google Play,

00:38:28.273 --> 00:38:29.690
and basically it's
the same thing.

00:38:29.690 --> 00:38:31.780
It's the same infrastructure
that hosts millions of apps

00:38:31.780 --> 00:38:33.220
already for the
Play Store, but this

00:38:33.220 --> 00:38:34.387
is for enterprise customers.

00:38:34.387 --> 00:38:37.150
And so imagine on a
fully-managed device,

00:38:37.150 --> 00:38:39.648
IT curates what applications
are available to the users,

00:38:39.648 --> 00:38:41.440
so you can define the
list of applications.

00:38:41.440 --> 00:38:43.030
And this covers that
example of like users trying

00:38:43.030 --> 00:38:45.363
to do their own thing with
their own set of applications

00:38:45.363 --> 00:38:46.572
for work-related activity.

00:38:46.572 --> 00:38:48.280
This will help solve
that problem, right?

00:38:48.280 --> 00:38:51.647
You can push out applications
that you deem are appropriate,

00:38:51.647 --> 00:38:53.980
whether they're available
through the Play Store already

00:38:53.980 --> 00:38:56.290
or you just deploy
enterprise applications.

00:38:56.290 --> 00:38:59.208
And you can also push them--

00:38:59.208 --> 00:39:00.625
like without the
user intervention

00:39:00.625 --> 00:39:03.042
and remove them from the device
without user intervention.

00:39:03.042 --> 00:39:05.080
Set update policies for
them if you wanted to.

00:39:05.080 --> 00:39:07.000
I heard a customer example
where they actually said,

00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:08.020
we don't want auto updates.

00:39:08.020 --> 00:39:09.520
We're really worried
because we have

00:39:09.520 --> 00:39:12.357
to vet these apps in advance,
and certain app developers

00:39:12.357 --> 00:39:14.190
are so agile, they
release updates every day

00:39:14.190 --> 00:39:15.412
and we just can't keep up.

00:39:15.412 --> 00:39:16.870
And so you can
control that, right?

00:39:16.870 --> 00:39:18.850
So that's the benefit
of Managed Google Play.

00:39:18.850 --> 00:39:20.850
And then with the work
profile, it's even better

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:22.390
because you can
let the user have

00:39:22.390 --> 00:39:24.557
a personal instance of
Play on the personal side,

00:39:24.557 --> 00:39:26.890
let them install whatever
they want from the Play Store,

00:39:26.890 --> 00:39:28.840
and then you have
the work side, right?

00:39:28.840 --> 00:39:32.390
So you control their work apps,
you ensure that, for example,

00:39:32.390 --> 00:39:35.500
if they can only use
one document repository,

00:39:35.500 --> 00:39:37.690
they can't install their
personal one into that side

00:39:37.690 --> 00:39:40.300
and share data between the two,
so they can't take their work

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:42.133
data with them somewhere
else and work on it

00:39:42.133 --> 00:39:44.718
with their own
preferred application.

00:39:44.718 --> 00:39:46.510
So it provides that
really nice separation.

00:39:46.510 --> 00:39:48.520
And then there's also a thing
called Manage App Configuration

00:39:48.520 --> 00:39:50.350
where historically
what you would do

00:39:50.350 --> 00:39:52.707
is like an enterprise app
or-- an app developer that's

00:39:52.707 --> 00:39:54.790
in the App Store but targets
Enterprise would say,

00:39:54.790 --> 00:39:55.060
you know what?

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:56.518
I need to build
like customizations

00:39:56.518 --> 00:39:57.400
for every customer.

00:39:57.400 --> 00:39:59.500
Well with Manage App
Config, they basically

00:39:59.500 --> 00:40:01.840
provide the hooks, and
then through the EMM,

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:03.640
you can basically
define whether you

00:40:03.640 --> 00:40:05.260
want to turn some
bells and whistles

00:40:05.260 --> 00:40:06.760
off in their specific
application, right?

00:40:06.760 --> 00:40:09.218
So it generalizes that, so it
could apply to any Enterprise

00:40:09.218 --> 00:40:11.680
customer without having to
do all these various one-off

00:40:11.680 --> 00:40:13.570
configurations.

00:40:13.570 --> 00:40:18.040
So like I mentioned earlier, one
of the things we really look at

00:40:18.040 --> 00:40:21.790
is, are our security
investments paying off, right?

00:40:21.790 --> 00:40:23.740
And it's really important.

00:40:23.740 --> 00:40:25.480
We call it measurable
security, right?

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:27.620
How do we measure success
in what we're doing?

00:40:27.620 --> 00:40:30.490
And so Gartner publishes
a comprehensive mobile OS

00:40:30.490 --> 00:40:32.090
comparison report every year.

00:40:32.090 --> 00:40:34.090
This is actually the
previous year's report.

00:40:34.090 --> 00:40:36.140
The new one is coming out
imminently-- actually,

00:40:36.140 --> 00:40:38.470
we were hoping it came out
before this, but it didn't.

00:40:38.470 --> 00:40:42.640
And so they have 28 categories,
and it's about 16 categories

00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:45.020
of built-in security
features that just

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:46.300
should be built into a phone.

00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:50.560
And these could include,
for example, OS updates,

00:40:50.560 --> 00:40:55.000
app updates, network
security, network encryption,

00:40:55.000 --> 00:40:57.160
authentication security,
exploit protection.

00:40:57.160 --> 00:40:59.025
And then on the
Enterprise controls,

00:40:59.025 --> 00:41:00.400
it's the stuff
you would expect--

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:03.800
key management, authentication
management, policy management,

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:04.930
and so forth.

00:41:04.930 --> 00:41:09.070
Anyways, as you can see, if you
look at the orange rectangle,

00:41:09.070 --> 00:41:11.650
you'll see that the
way they scored this

00:41:11.650 --> 00:41:14.440
was basically weak,
average, strong, right?

00:41:14.440 --> 00:41:15.610
Low, medium, high.

00:41:15.610 --> 00:41:18.047
And what you'll see is that
the three devices or the three

00:41:18.047 --> 00:41:20.380
columns that scored the highest
were all Android, right?

00:41:20.380 --> 00:41:24.340
So it was Android Oreo, it
was the Pixel 2 running Oreo,

00:41:24.340 --> 00:41:26.380
and it was a Samsung
device running Oreo, right?

00:41:26.380 --> 00:41:28.005
They scored the
highest in this report.

00:41:28.005 --> 00:41:30.490
Specifically the
Pixel scored strong

00:41:30.490 --> 00:41:32.800
in 24 out of 28 categories.

00:41:32.800 --> 00:41:38.320
And iOS, you can see
yourselves, scored 12 strong out

00:41:38.320 --> 00:41:39.610
of 28 categories, right?

00:41:39.610 --> 00:41:41.760
So this is how we
measure success, right?

00:41:41.760 --> 00:41:45.100
We want to make sure that
analysts are taking a look

00:41:45.100 --> 00:41:47.150
and evaluating what we provide.

00:41:47.150 --> 00:41:51.490
So anyways, let's quickly recap.

00:41:51.490 --> 00:41:54.240
I don't really need
this slide, but just

00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:56.050
save some time for questions.

00:41:56.050 --> 00:41:58.110
But basically, again, I
tried to do it a little

00:41:58.110 --> 00:42:01.740
differently today just
covering system-related risk,

00:42:01.740 --> 00:42:04.950
app-related risk, things that
we can help with phishing

00:42:04.950 --> 00:42:06.240
that's just built in.

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:10.066
And the end user is the
biggest risk, right?

00:42:10.066 --> 00:42:12.760
And so thank you very much.

00:42:12.760 --> 00:42:15.100
One thing to point out--
if you're interested,

00:42:15.100 --> 00:42:16.600
if you want to get
more information,

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:17.895
we recently refreshed
our security site,

00:42:17.895 --> 00:42:19.562
it's called the Android
Security Center.

00:42:19.562 --> 00:42:21.000
It's android.com/security.

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:23.730
And since this is the
last one of the day,

00:42:23.730 --> 00:42:26.460
please be generous and
be nice on the surveys.

00:42:26.460 --> 00:42:29.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

